TI  - Spatial and temporal analysis of reasonable livestock carrying capacity and ecological carrying capacity of grasslands in pastoral areas of China
T2  - CHINESE SCIENCE BULLETIN-CHINESE
AB  - Grassland pastoral areas are important ecological security barriers and livestock production zones in China. Clarifying the ecological carrying status and changing trend of grassland pastoral areas can provide scientific basis for the implementation of grass-based livestock management, grassland ecological protection and restoration, and the construction of an important ecological security barrier in northern China. In this paper, we calculated the reasonable livestock carrying capacity of grassland and its changing trend in 268 pastoral and semi-pastoral counties in China from 2000 to 2020 by utilizing multi-temporal remote sensing monitoring data, such as net primary productivity, together with the theory of compensatory growth of plants and the reasonable grazing intensity for different grassland types. We further classified the ecological carrying capacity of grassland in pastoral and semi-pastoral areas by combining the number of livestock and the supplemental feeding rate of the statistical yearbook. The results showed that (1) the average yield of the natural grassland in pastoral and semi-pastoral counties from 2000 to 2020 was 656 million tons/year, and the utilizable grassland yield was 225 million tons/year. Among them, the utilizable grass yield of natural grassland in pastoral counties is 158 million tons/ year, accounting for 70.17% of the total supply, and the utilizable grass yield of natural grassland in semi-pastoral counties is 672,464,400 tons/year, accounting for 29.83% of the total supply. (2) spatially, the reasonable carrying capacity of grassland in China ' s grassland pastoral areas was significantly differentiated by the water-heat gradient, showing a spatial pattern of decreasing from east to west and from south to north. (3) temporally, the reasonable livestock-carrying capacity of grasslands in Nei Mongol and other northeastern areas showed an overall increasing trend from 2000 to 2020, but the reasonable livestock-carrying capacity of grasslands in southwestern Xizang and northwestern Xinjiang showed an overall decreasing trend. (4) the number of livestock in pastoral and semi-pastoral counties increased by an average of 4,434,500 sheep units per year, with a total increase of 93,124,300 sheep units. (5) with considering the wild animals in pastoral areas, the overloading rate in pastoral areas of the country amounted to 91.42%, and the degree of overloading in semi-pastoral counties was higher than that in pastoral counties, in which 87.70% of pastoral counties were overloaded, while 94.52% of semi-pastoral counties were overloaded; in contrast, without considering the wild animals in pastoral areas, the overall overloading rate decreased to 46.28%, and the proportion of overloading among pastoral counties and semi-pastoral counties decreased to 33.61% and 51.37%, respectively. (6) during 2000-2020, only 49 pastoral and semi-pastoral counties in China had changes in ecological carrying status of grassland, of which 30 counties showed an increase while 19 showed a decrease. Our findings are of practical significance in guiding the scientific configuration of grassland ecological-productive functions and realizing the sustainable development of grass and pasture industry in China.
