TI  - BIOCHEMICAL AND MICROBIOLOGICAL PROPERTIES OF A WEST-COAST WET LAND SOIL AT DIFFERENT STAGES OF PASTURE DEVELOPMENT
T2  - NEW ZEALAND JOURNAL OF SCIENCE
AB  - A study was made of biochemical and microbiological properties of the topsoil of Okarito silt loam (a gley podzol) at 4 sites [South Island, New Zealand] that had been under-fertilized, grazed and introduced pastures of grasses and legumes for 1, 3, 7 and 13 yr. The soil at these sites previously supported sedges, ferns, and scrub (pakihi) vegetation. The pH of the soil increased from 4.6 to 5.3 with pasture age and the C/N ratios decreased non-significantly from 22 to 19. Organic matter contents were generally high and variable within and between sites and did not change noticeably with time. Respiratory activity (CO2 production) and indices of microbial biomass (biomass C and mineral-N flush) were roughly proportional to the soil organic C or total N contents. On an organic C basis, invertase activity increased with pasture age, and amylase and hemicellulase activities showed similar but less marked trends. Cellulase, urease, phosphatase and sulphatase activities did not show any trends with pasture age. Net mineralization of soil organic N (on a total soil N basis), and nitrification of the ammonium-N produced, both increased with pasture age, from very low levels in the 1 yr pasture samples to moderately high levels, with mainly nitrate-N produced, in the oldest pasture samples. Both quantitative and qualitative changes in the microbial population of the soil were apparent as a result of development. Numbers of bacteria and actinomycetes increased with pasture age, and numbers of fungi correspondingly declined.
