TI  - BIOCHEMICAL-CHANGES WITH PASTURE DEVELOPMENT IN A WEST-COAST WET LAND SOIL - A NOTE
T2  - NEW ZEALAND JOURNAL OF SCIENCE
AB  - Biochemical properties were measured in the topsoil of Okarito silt loam (a gley podzol) at a site that was initially under sedges, ferns, and scrub (pakihi) vegetation, and subsequently under a fertilized, grazed pasture of introduced grasses and legumes. Samples were taken .apprx. 6 mo. before development (0 yr), and 6 mo. and 4.5 yr after development (1 and 5 yr). The pH of the soil had increased from 4.2 in the 0-year samples to 5.2 in the 5-yr samples. Organic matter contents and C/N ratios were very variable and had not changed significantly with time. On an organic C basis, the rate of CO2 production had increased between 1 and 5 yr, whereas estimates of soil microbial biomass (biomass C and mineral-N flush) had declined. This decrease in microbial biomass may, however, have been partly a consequence of processing the much wetter 5-year samples. On the organic C basis, soil invertase, amylase, cellulase and sulfatase activities increased and urease activity decreased significantly with pasture age; hemicellulase and phosphatase activities had not changed significantly. Net mineralization and nitrification of soil organic N were negligible, or at very low values in the 0- and 1-yr samples, but increased markedly with pasture age. Results generally confirm those found previously with samples of Okarito soils from pastures of differnt ages at different sites.
