AU  - VANDYNE, GM
TI  - PREDICTION BY NONLINEAR PROGRAMMING OF RELATIVE CHEMICAL COMPOSITION OF DIETARY BOTANICAL COMPONENTS
T2  - JOURNAL OF RANGE MANAGEMENT
AB  - This paper shows by example how recently-developed, non-statistical mathematical techniques can be used to develop nutritional information about chemical composition of individual plants and plant parts. Nonlinear programming techniques are introduced and used to predict relative chemical composition and energy values of plants or plant parts in dietary samples obtained from esophageally fistulated cattle and sheep. This information is predicted from estimates of the weight composition of botanical components and from chemical composition of the entire sample. The results are discussed with respect to tests of the method and with respect to implications for knowledge of grazing performance of cattle and sheep on summer annual rangelands. Results obtained by these mathematical analyses would be difficult or impossible to obtain by direct experimentation. These studies and recent use of environmental biologists of other new analytical techniques illustrate the need for more training for resource scientists in mathematical, statistical, and computer sciences.
