TI  - PHOSPHORUS AND BEEF-PRODUCTION IN NORTHERN AUSTRALIA .7. THE EFFECT OF PHOSPHORUS ON THE COMPOSITION, YIELD AND QUALITY OF LEGUME-BASED PASTURE AND THEIR RELATION TO ANIMAL PRODUCTION
T2  - TROPICAL GRASSLANDS
AB  - Four grazing experiments were conducted in northern Australia to study the effect of P as fertilizer or supplement, on pasture and animal production.  At 2 sites, Katherine and Springmount, legume was sown into the perennial grass savanna while at Lansdown and Narayen the trees were removed and grass and legume sown.  At each site the inherent low soil P status, combined with different rates of P fertilizer application, produced a range of soil fertility treatments.  Data from the 4 sites were combined to investigate the effect of P on the development, yield, botanical composition and quality of legume based pasture and to relate cattle liveweight responses to pasture attributes influenced by P inputs.
   Fertilizer increased the yield of pasture at all sites.  The influence of fertilizer on botanical composition varied between sites and was largely dependent on the inherent soil P status and the species introduced.  At Springmount and Lansdown, a single small application of P fertilizer had a large effect on the botanical composition which persisted well beyond a measurable effect on soil P.
   Plant P concentration increased with increasing rate of P fertilizer but concentrations were below animal requirement, except at high rates of fertilizer application.  Nitrogen concentration was sometimes increased as a result of improved P nutrition but the effect was not consistent across species or across sites.  Seasonal fluctuations in P and N concentrations were large and were greater in grass than in legume.  Herbage calcium to phosphorus ratios declined with increasing inputs of fertilizer.
   Liveweight gain was closely related to plant P concentration and fertilizer rate at Springmount and Narayen.
