AU  - Morgan, JW
AU  - Fensham, RJ
AU  - Godfree, R
AU  - Foreman, PW
A1  - Keith, DA
TI  - Australian Tussock Grasslands
T2  - AUSTRALIAN VEGETATION, 3RD EDITION
AB  - In this review, we highlight the evolution, distribution and ecological features of tussock grasslands throughout Australia. Grassland distribution appears to be largely driven by bottom-up processes (i. e. climate-edaphic factors), although there are some situations where fire has likely played an important role, and derived grasslands are now widespread due to clearing and grazing. The strong relationship of grasslands with fertile, clay soils, developed for agriculture since European arrival, is one of the key reasons why they are now reduced across much of their natural range. Plant diversity in tussock grasslands has long been thought to be promoted by disturbance (e. g. fire), although it is apparent that the paucity of experimental studies at appropriate spatial scales, especially stratified across climateregulated productivity gradients, hinders our confidence in this prediction. Additionally, the almost complete lack of climate change experiments in tussock grasslands limits our ability to predict its likely impacts on ecosystem composition, structure and function. There have been substantial gains in the preservation of tussock grasslands over the last two decades, and changes in land use offer opportunities for passive restoration. Fragmented tussock grasslands are currently subjected to numerous ecosystem stresses and these will likely have a big impact on future tussock grassland resilience and stability.
