TI  - IMPACT OF MARSH DEGRADATION ON AQUATIC ECOSYSTEM IN THE ZOIGE MARSH, QINGHAI-TIBETAN PLATEAU
T2  - 11TH INTERNATIONAL SYMPOSIUM ON ECOHYDRAULICS
CP  - 11th International Symposium on Ecohydraulics
AB  - The Zoige Marsh, located in the Northeastern Qianghai-Tibetan Plateau, is the largest highland marsh in the world. It harbors many endemic and endangered species and behaves as the major water source to the headstream of the Yellow River. However, due to climate change and misuses of the marsh, such as drainage, peat exploitation, and livestock grazing, the Zoige Marsh has suffered severe ecological stresses in the last decades. This study presents findings of systematic investigations and analyses on water quality and biodiversity of macroinvertebrate assemblages of the running water channels, wetlands, and drained regions of the Zoige Marsh. It was found that there was organic pollution in the Zoige Plateau even though it is far remote from urbanization, owing to the heavy grazing. Analyses of macroinvertebrate assemblages indicated that the lotic river channels preserved the highest taxa richness of macroinvertebrate assemblages, followed by the swamps, and the dried marsh regions had the lowest taxa richness. It is clear that the drainage of Zoige Marsh has resulted degradation of aquatic biodiversity to a large extent. Implications for ecological restoration and biodiversity protection of the Zoige Marsh are discussed.
