AU  - LEBARON, A
AU  - BOND, LK
AU  - AITKEN, P
AU  - MICHAELSEN, L
TI  - EXPLANATION OF THE BOLIVIAN HIGHLANDS GRAZING REVERSIBLE EROSION SYNDROME
T2  - JOURNAL OF RANGE MANAGEMENT
AB  - Highland Bolivia is naturally subject to heavy erosion. The situation is exacerbated by the presenceof denuded ranges. The large bands of sheep and goats, which cause overgrazing, are on or close to the biological limit of the range resource. Why this should be is a mystery to outsiders, since all available grazing is subject to private or communal control. That an adequate explanation can be found in free competition for a common property resource is rejected mainly because what appears to be common rangeland is not. An explanation for overstocking is found in a complex of factors linked to culture and tradition as well as to agronomic forces. Typical pressure for families to maintain herd sizes is reinforced by little preception of erosion as a threat to subsistence and by lack of forage alternatives in bad years. Even at the community level little incentive exists to voluntarily reduce grazing. Nonvoluntary control options are discussed, but all would be difficult to introduce. This case study is an illustration of the kind of background knowlege that must be developed to combat erosion in Third World settings.
