AU  - MCCORKLE, JS
TI  - RANCHING IN PANAMA
T2  - JOURNAL OF RANGE MANAGEMENT
AB  - Beef and dairy cattle are produced on grass in Panama. Few operators add any supplemental feed. Grazing land is derived from natural grassland, savannah and tropical forest. Ninety percent of the grazing land has been improved by adding introduced grasses. Principal exotics axe Hyparrhenia, Panicum, and Pen-nisetum in one or more species. Principal native grasses include species of Paspalum, Andropogon, Axonopus and Panicum. Mineral deficiencies, including Ca and P, occur on a very high percentage of soils. Serious nutritional deficiencies occur in the dry season. Other problems are disease and parasites, inadequate culling, lack of quality in breeding stock, and other inadequacies. The result is poor calf crops and low production per breeding animal.
