AU  - MCCOSKER, TH
TI  - ESTABLISHMENT AND PERSISTENCE OF INTRODUCED GRASSES ON THE MARRAKAI LAND SYSTEM, IN THE NORTHERN TERRITORY SEMIARID TROPICS
T2  - TROPICAL GRASSLANDS
AB  - A range of 26 introduced grasses were sown on typical soils on "Mt. Bundey" station in the northern Territory, and grazed to evaluate establishment, persistence and adaptation. Prepared seedbeds were used with ample fertilizer and a common suite of legumes. This paper also reports on commercial experience gained during and subsequent to the experimental period. Well adapted grasses were Andropogon gayanus cv. Kent, Setaria sphacelata cv. Kazungula, Brachiaria humidicola cv. Tully and Digitaria decumbens (pangola). Grasses such as Paspalum plicatulum cv. Bryan, Panicum maximum cvv. Hamil, Coloniao and common guinea, and Bothriochloa pertusa cv. Bowen were adapted only to certain soil and management conditions. Brachiaria decumbens cv. Basilisk, B. mutica (para grass) and Paspalum plicatulum cv. Rodds Bay are adapted to other soils in the district but did not persist on "Mt. Bundey". Grasses not well adapted to the soils and tropical monsoonal environment were  Cenchrus ciliaris cvv. Gayndah and American, C. setigerus cv. Birdwood, Panicum maximum cv. Petrie (green panic), Setaria sphacelata cv. Narok, Chloris gayana cv. Callide, Urochloa mosambicensis cv. Nixon and CPI 46876, U. bolbodes CPI 47122, and Bothriochloa insculpta cv. Hatch. Insufficient observations were made on Hyparrhenia rufa CPI''s 52255, 52256, 52257 and Q 5108 to assess their potential. Stylosanthes scabra cv. Seca and S. hamata cv. Verano were adapted and useful legumes. Calopogonium mucunoides (calopo) was vigorous on yellow earth soils but was poorly eaten by stock. S. guianensis cv. Cook and Macrotyloma axillare cv. Archer were not adapted. Successful commercial pastures were developed on "Mt. Bundey" using: (a) Kent on skeletal lithosol and shallow yellow earth soils with Verano; (b) Kazungula on deep yellow earths with Verano; (c) Tully or Kazungula on soloth/solodic flood plains with Bundey Centurion; and (d) Hamil on levee soils following calopo. These have proved more viable mixtures than those previously available.
