AU  - OCUJPAUGH, WR
AU  - QUESENBERRY, KH
AU  - MOORE, JE
TI  - MOB-GRAZING OF MORPHOLOGICALLY DIFFERENT AESCHYNOMENE SPECIES
T2  - TROPICAL GRASSLANDS
AB  - The tropical annual legume Aeschynomene americana L. has potential for use in wetland tropical pastures, and information is needed regarding forage potential of other members of the genus. Two studies were conducted using the mob-grazing technique to study effects of animal defoliation on regrowth and forage quality of morphologically diverse Aeschynomene spp. Both experiments were conducted on a wet Flatwoods soil of the Pomona series (sandy, siliceous hyperthermic Ultic Haplaquod), northeast of Gainesville, FL [USA]. The 1980 study evaluated several plant introductions from 4 Aeschynomene species. Cattle selected leaf, seed, and fine stem material less than 4 mm in diameter. Leaf + seed crude protein (CP) ranged from 150 to 300 g/kg compared to 100 g/kg for stem CP. In vitro organic matter digestibility (IVOMD) was 650 to 750 and 300 to 400 g/kg for leaf + seed and stem fractions, respectively. The 1981 experiment considered effect of plant maturity (as affected by  date of initial grazing) and plant density on growth and utilization of Florida common aeschynomene. Pastures grazed earliest (July 27) showed vigorous regrowth-throughout the season. There were differences between pregrazing leaf + seed and stem fractions of greater than 300 g/kg for IVOMD and 150 g/kg for CP. Forage quality of postgrazing residue resembled that of the pregrazing stem fraction. Plant density effects interacted with initial grazing date and grazing cycle to influence dry matter  production. These preliminary studies suggest that although clipping trials provide valuable agronomic information, the factor of animal selectivity should be involved in early evaluation of forages to be utilized primarily as pasture species.
